WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.608
Performance means doing the real task under normal conditions.

2
00:00:03.608 --> 00:00:11.224
You troubleshoot the production issue, deliver the presentation, write the report, lead the meeting, or make the operational decision.

3
00:00:11.224 --> 00:00:12.426
Practice is different.

4
00:00:12.426 --> 00:00:16.835
Practice isolates parts of the task so they can be improved.

5
00:00:16.835 --> 00:00:23.650
It creates room to slow down, repeat, compare, and correct without the full pressure of live performance.

6
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:26.856
People often confuse being busy with getting better.

7
00:00:26.856 --> 00:00:33.270
A busy day may include many opportunities to perform, but performance pressure can make learning harder.

8
00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:38.481
In production work, people are trying to finish, avoid mistakes, and meet commitments.

9
00:00:38.481 --> 00:00:43.692
They may not have time to examine the weak part of the skill.

10
00:00:43.692 --> 00:00:50.506
Practice time lets them step away from pure output and work on the mechanism behind better output.

11
00:00:50.506 --> 00:00:56.920
Safe practice environments can include rehearsal, drills, simulations, worked examples, targeted exercises, and low-stakes review.

12
00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:03.734
A developer might practice threat modeling on a small example before doing it on a major system.

13
00:01:03.734 --> 00:01:08.143
A manager might rehearse a difficult conversation before the real meeting.

14
00:01:08.143 --> 00:01:13.354
An analyst might compare several decision records to identify where assumptions were weak.

15
00:01:13.354 --> 00:01:18.966
Separating learning time from production pressure does not mean practice is artificial or irrelevant.

16
00:01:18.966 --> 00:01:25.780
Good practice is connected to real work, but it is designed for improvement instead of immediate delivery.

17
00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:36.603
The point is to create a space where mistakes are visible, feedback is usable, and the learner can repeat the critical part before the stakes are higher.
